UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3: Read and count whole numbers', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Whole numbers count complete units. We read them by naming place groups from ones upward, not by treating every digit as ones.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nHow many tens make one hundred? How many hundreds make one thousand?\n\nFEEDBACK\nTen tens make one hundred. Ten hundreds make one thousand.\n\nWATCH FOR\nReading 4,518 as \"four, five, one, eight ones\" ignores place value.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nFor 3,706 and 2,540: name each digit''s place and value. Then count forward by ones from 3,698 to 3,705, writing the numbers. Explain one regrouping you used.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Whole numbers count complete units. We read them by naming place groups from ones upward, not by treating every digit as ones.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Concept","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-RN-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: For 3,706 and 2,540: name each digit''s place and value. Then count forward by ones from 3,698 to 3,705, writing the numbers. Explain one regrouping you used. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Ten tens make one hundred. Ten hundreds make one thousand. Reasoning: I used whole number by attending to place and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Reading 4,518 as \"four, five, one, eight ones\" ignores place value. Correction: Ten tens make one hundred. Ten hundreds make one thousand. Why: the correction restores accurate use of whole number.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Reading 4,518 as \"four, five, one, eight ones\" ignores place value. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses whole number with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with whole number, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require new numbers, visible working and named places/values. / Require a real misconception, corrected working and a method reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Ten tens make one hundred. Ten hundreds make one thousand. This matches the lesson skill for whole number.","options":["Reading 4,518 as \"four, five, one, eight ones\" ignores place value.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Ten tens make one hundred. Ten hundreds make one thousand."],"question":"How many tens make one hundred? How many hundreds make one thousand?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Reading 4,518 as \"four, five, one, eight ones\" ignores place value. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Whole numbers count complete units. We read them by naming place groups from ones upward, not by treating every digit as ones.","Ten tens make one hundred. Ten hundreds make one thousand.","Careful use of place and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Reading 4,518 as \"four, five, one, eight ones\" ignores place value."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising whole number?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named place and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the place and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right."],"question":"A learner is practising whole number. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value or quantity.","options":["Change digits to look neater without checking place value.","Reading 4,518 as \"four, five, one, eight ones\" ignores place value.","Show the places, values or steps and say why each step is valid.","Write only a final number with no working."],"question":"Which working note best supports a mathematics answer about whole number?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the method; the others break it.","options":["Ten tens make one hundred. Ten hundreds make one thousand.","Reading 4,518 as \"four, five, one, eight ones\" ignores place value.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Whole numbers count complete units. We read them by naming place groups from ones upward, not by treating… — which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Disagreement is resolved by checking places, operations and the question focus.","options":["Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed.","Pick the longer-looking working automatically.","Delete units so the answers appear to match.","Average the two answers without checking either."],"question":"Which error should you repair first if two methods disagree?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same method.","options":["Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Solve a fresh problem with different numbers and explain the method.","Retype the worked model numbers as the answer.","Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers."],"question":"Which practice choice shows transfer of whole number?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Useful feedback names the failed step and shows corrected working.","options":["Just add more digits until it looks longer.","Name the exact place or step that failed, then show corrected working for whole number.","Praise neat handwriting even when the places are wrong.","Tell the learner to skip working and only keep the final digits."],"question":"Which feedback comment is most useful after a mistake?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require new numbers, visible working and named places/values.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses whole number with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Ten tens make one hundred. Ten hundreds make one thousand. Reasoning: I used whole number by attending to place and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require a real misconception, corrected working and a method reason.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with whole number, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Reading 4,518 as \"four, five, one, eight ones\" ignores place value. Correction: Ten tens make one hundred. Ten hundreds make one thousand. Why: the correction restores accurate use of whole number.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y3-w01-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise whole number.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Number and Algebra","success_criteria":["I can use whole number with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify place and explain why it matters.","I can practise ones independently on a new prompt.","I can notice tens needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Whole numbers count complete units. We read them by naming place groups from ones upward, not by treating every digit as ones. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working.","Whole numbers count complete units. We read them by naming place groups from ones upward, not by treating every digit as ones.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about whole number?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n4,518 has 8 ones, 1 ten, 5 hundreds and 4 thousands. The digit 5 means 500 because it sits in the hundreds place.\n\nTRY FIRST\nHow many tens make one hundred? How many hundreds make one thousand?\n\nFEEDBACK\nTen tens make one hundred. Ten hundreds make one thousand.","explain":"Whole numbers count complete units. We read them by naming place groups from ones upward, not by treating every digit as ones.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Reading 4,518 as \"four, five, one, eight ones\" ignores place value. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Place chart for four thousand five hundred eighteen.","caption":"A digit''s column gives its value.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Number</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">4,518</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Places</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">4 thousands; 5 hundreds; 1 ten; 8 ones</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: 4,518 has 8 ones, 1 ten, 5 hundreds and 4 thousands. The digit 5 means 500 because it sits in the hundreds place. Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: 4,518 has 8 ones, 1 ten, 5 hundreds and 4 thousands. The digit 5 means 500 because it sits in the hundreds place. Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working.","Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about whole number?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n4,518 has 8 ones, 1 ten, 5 hundreds and 4 thousands. The digit 5 means 500 because it sits in the hundreds place. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Equal grouping into the next place.","caption":"Places grow by tens of the place to the right.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Ten tens</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">one hundred</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Ten hundreds</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">one thousand</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A guess with no working.","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about whole number?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nReading 4,518 as \"four, five, one, eight ones\" ignores place value. Here is a more productive model: 4,518 has 8 ones, 1 ten, 5 hundreds and 4 thousands. The digit 5 means 500 because it sits in the hundreds place.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then write corrected working.\n\nFEEDBACK\nTen tens make one hundred. Ten hundreds make one thousand.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Reading 4,518 as \"four, five, one, eight ones\" ignores place value. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about whole number?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nWhole numbers count complete units. We read them by naming place groups from ones upward, not by treating every digit as ones. Now choose different numbers to show you can transfer the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSolve one fresh problem that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your answer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers.","explain":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new numbers. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Transfer to a new problem"}],"title":"Year 3: Read and count whole numbers","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Whole numbers and place value","week":1,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\n4,518 has 8 ones, 1 ten, 5 hundreds and 4 thousands. The digit 5 means 500 because it sits in the hundreds place.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the places, values or distances used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Place chart for four thousand five hundred eighteen.","caption":"A digit''s column gives its value.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Number</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">4,518</text><text x=\"358\" y=